  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 PM MDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nHOT AND DRY THROUGH THIS WEEKEND:\n\nBuilding high pressure centered to our West is keeping the\nweather hot and dry today. The center of high pressure will\ngradually move to our southeast over the next few days, but as \nit moves overhead during this transition hot and dry conditions \nremain in the forecast through the weekend. High temperatures \nare expected to be around 10 degrees above normal today and \ntomorrow, with a decrease of a few degrees forecast for Sunday.\nOvernight lows in the desert valley areas will be in the upper \n60s to low 70s tonight, and then in the low to mid 70s tomorrow \nnight into Sunday morning thanks to increased overnight cloud \ncover. So, Heat Advisories remain in effect this weekend for \nsome desert valley areas due to near record daytime high \ntemperatures, and elevated overnight temperatures. \n\nDespite the current hot and dry weather, widespread critical \nfire weather conditions remain out of the forecast through at \nleast much of next week due to weaker wind gusts while high\npressure is overhead, and an eventual return of moisture. \nHowever, localized critical fire weather conditions are possible\nin some areas, partially due to widespread single digit \nrelative humidity values today and tomorrow, with poor \novernight recoveries tonight. \n\nMOISTURE RETURN NEXT WEEK: \n\nGradually the center of high pressure will move more over the  \nSouth Central Plains, which will allow for moisture to advect\ninto the Southwest, including our region. Chances of showers \nand thunderstorms increases on Sunday with the initial onset of \nmoisture, but very dry surface conditions and limited forcing \nwill make it difficult for rain to reach the ground. Each day \nPoPs and QPF will increase and become more widespread as \nmoisture continues to advect into the region and a few \nshortwaves move within the flow. There is still a large amount \nof uncertainty regarding the magnitude of this moisture push, \nbut models continue to favor a midweek jump in QPF.",
